1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:08,050
Um, so imagine spending millions of dollars designing, you know, the perfect blueprint for a massive new digital infrastructure.

2
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,190
Right. And then the project teams just completely ignore it.

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,300
Exactly. They just build whatever they want anyway.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:16,080
I mean, in government IT, that happens every single day.

5
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:26,100
Mm. So welcome to the Deep Dive. Today, we're looking at how to transform your national enterprise architecture from a, a theoretical document into the actual command

6
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:28,650
center of digital decision-making for your ministry.

7
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:34,720
Yeah, because having a beautiful architecture on paper is-- Well, it's pretty useless if you don't have the teeth to enforce it.

8
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:41,060
Right. I mean, the repository holds the architecture, sure, but the governance board is what actually gives it authority.

9
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:47,500
Without that board, your architecture is just a document that people can and absolutely will ignore.

10
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:50,180
Yeah. And then you just end up with what they call governance theater.

11
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:56,580
Exactly. Governance theater. Yeah. Where you're just, you know, producing meeting minutes instead of making real decisions.

12
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:05,410
Wow. Okay. So if your ministry is setting up its, uh, PAERA, your Pan-European Architecture framework,

13
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:08,020
you're gonna be dealing with state registers.

14
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:17,220
Right. And establishing shared building blocks, but none of that really matters if the board overseeing it all doesn't have actual power, right?

15
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:22,660
No, it doesn't matter at all. To make an enterprise architecture work, you need a board that can actually say no.

16
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:25,440
Okay, so let's start there, the power to say no.

17
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,640
Yeah. This is the critical shift. The board must be binding.

18
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:30,280
Not advisory. Binding.

19
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:31,740
Because an advisory board just-

20
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:38,860
It just looks at a project, offers a polite opinion, and then the project team says thanks and goes ahead and does whatever they originally planned anyway.

21
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:39,200
Got it. Right.

22
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:43,960
But a binding board makes its rulings a hard condition of proceeding.

23
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,790
So if a project is told to, say,

24
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,970
consume a shared identity platform, it just does so, or it gets zero funding.

25
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:52,460
Exactly. Zero funding.

26
00:01:52,480 --> 00:02:00,160
But wait, I wanna challenge that a bit because if you give a board absolute binding authority over every single IT project in a government,

27
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:03,320
aren't you just creating a massive bureaucratic choke point?

28
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:06,240
Uh, yeah. That is a very common concern.

29
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:13,480
Like, how do we prevent this from becoming a rigid bottleneck that just slows down vital government services?

30
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:21,700
Well, that is the exact fear, and a bottleneck usually happens because leaders are afraid to make a final call, which just leads to endless debate.

31
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:26,300
Right. So that's why who holds the power matters just as much as the power itself.

32
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:31,960
I see. Who chairs the board dictates whether a no actually sticks quickly and decisively.

33
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:36,100
So it really can't just be, like, a mid-level manager running things.

34
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:41,500
Oh, definitely not. The chair must be your chief digitalization officer or the minister directly.

35
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:42,960
Wow, the minister directly.

36
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:50,290
Yeah. They need to be senior enough that when a powerful sector ministry brings in their, you know, favorite pet project-

37
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,989
Yeah, the one they really wanna push through.

38
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:58,260
Exactly. And the board declines it, that decision has to hold up against severe political pressure.

39
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,049
Okay. Let me see if I have this right. It's like

40
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:03,380
the architect isn't the one grabbing the steering wheel here.

41
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:07,049
Yeah. They're just the GPS. They're saying, "Hey, here's the analysis.

42
00:03:07,109 --> 00:03:07,680
Wrote this way."

43
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:15,299
But the chair, meaning the minister or CDO, they are the ones actually driving the car and hitting the brakes on a bad project.

44
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,940
Precisely. The architect informs, but the chair decides.

45
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,520
Got it. But the chair isn't alone in that room.

46
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:28,400
You need the right membership. The room must consist of the people whose systems actually carry the decisions.

47
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:29,560
So who are we talking about here?

48
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:32,780
We're talking about sector ministry CIOs,

49
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:37,920
owners of major state registers, like the population or identity register,

50
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:42,200
the data protection regulator, and maybe one external advisor.

51
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:42,960
Keep it tight.

52
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:44,320
Keep it tight. So a small group.

53
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:48,700
Yeah. A board of the right 12 people is far better than a board of the convenient 40.

54
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:55,460
But why is that? I mean, I can imagine a ministry wanting absolutely everyone represented, so nobody feels left out of the loop.

55
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:59,620
Sure. But because when you have 40 people in a room, you don't get decisions, you get a seminar.

56
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:01,220
Oh, right. A seminar.

57
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:04,700
Yeah. Nobody wants to be the one to say no in front of a giant crowd.

58
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:12,700
It diffuses responsibility. But if you shrink it down to 12 people whose actual budgets and systems are on the line-

59
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:13,720
Then you get real votes.

60
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:17,659
Exactly. Suddenly, you get real binding votes.

61
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:23,680
That makes total sense. Mm. But bringing those 12 powerful people together kinda leads to the speed issue again.

62
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:27,040
I mean, having the right people only works if they meet at the right pace.

63
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:30,380
Right. And the cadence must govern without blocking.

64
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:31,620
Okay. How does that work?

65
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:38,720
You establish a standing rhythm. So quarterly main meetings to look at the big picture, what has changed, what to prioritize,

66
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:42,420
plus an ad hoc fast path for urgent project decisions.

67
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:47,180
Because if the board is too slow, I imagine projects will just route around it anyway to meet their own deadline.

68
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:53,480
Oh, definitely. And that is how you end up with systems that are fragmented, siloed, duplicated, and costly to change.

69
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:58,680
Exactly right. Binding authority plus responsiveness is what keeps projects coming through the front door.

70
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:04,610
I mean, redesigning government requires the business side and the IT side to sit down and make decisions together.

71
00:05:04,780 --> 00:05:07,210
Right. The repository provides the shared picture,

72
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:12,390
the meta model provides the shared words, and the board provides the shared rhythm.

73
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:19,560
Well, it sounds like setting up the shared rhythm requires a massive cultural shift across these traditionally siloed ministries.

74
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:20,320
It really does.

75
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:29,420
But it all hinges on that structure. So to succeed, your program must stand up a binding board chaired by a senior leader made of affected members

76
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:31,740
and operating on a responsive cadence.

77
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:35,120
Simply put, stand up a board that can truly say no.

78
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:42,220
That really clarifies the stakes. So if the repository is your shared picture, the meta model your shared words, and the board your shared rhythm,

